Evaristo,

  We experienced a very similar problem with an Alpha500 system at the Royal
Observatory in Scotland.  Our machine had 384 Mbytes, and when it was upgraded
to Digital Unix 4.0e, it would not boot.  Our failure message was exactly the
same as yours.

Using the information in your posting, we were able to successfully boot if we
set memlimit=300.

   The next day we were fortunate to get some technical phone support from
Compaq/Digital.  From the boot-up prompt he had us do:

        >>> set os_type vms
        >>> init
        ...
        >>> boot

This solved the problem!  He told us that when the os_type is Unix, the full
memory is not checked prior to boot, and this causes failures on some
systems. But telling the firmware that it will boot VMS causes all memory to
be checked, then booting (of Unix) begins from the disk.  He didn't say why
this needs to be done -- maybe it is parity memory and every address needs to
be written in order for the parity logic to be fully initialized.

  He also said we may experience one side effect on the next power-cycle.
Because vms mode and unix mode firmware store the date in a different
structure, he told us to expect that the year will show up as 2019.  But at
that point we are to use the Unix 'date' function to set the date properly,
and everything should be ok.


