Iwan,

Logical Storage Manager (LSM) provides tools to more effectively manage disk
storage
in today's demanding storage environment. Using virtual disks, called volumes, LSM

eases the difficulty of managing traditional UNIX[R] storage, especially for
growing
commercial systems with tens or hundreds of disks. Instead of using inadequate
tools to
manage standard physical devices, LSM gives you flexible tools to manage logical
volumes consisting of arbitrary areas of disks. Because these volumes transcend
the
limitations of native UNIX partitions and devices, LSM gives you more flexibility
to mold
storage resources according to the needs of applications, databases, and users.
Disks
can be added to or removed from volumes without shutting down the system. Spanning

builds virtual disks larger than the underlying physical disks. Mirroring provides
redundant
data if a disk fails, without interrupting active use of the system. Striping
increases
input/output (I/O) performance.

The Logical Storage Manager provides software volume (block and raw devices)
abstraction with DIGITAL UNIX. LSM provides:


   *      Data Spanning and partitioning
   *      Data Mirroring (RAID1)
   *      Data Striping (RAID0)
   *      Combined mirroring of a stripe set (RAID0 and RAID1)
   *      Comprehensive ONLINE data storage management:
        o           Add and remove disks
        o           Reconfigure volumes to change configuration
        o           Migrate data for disk repair or load balancing while data is
          in use
        o           Monitor Performance
        o           GUI and command line interfaces

Hope this help :-)

David Lee

