In article <36F67E26.D5D42477@caaisn.leeds.ac.uk>,
	m.daly@caaisn.leeds.ac.uk (Michael Daly) writes:
>I am currently running Digital UNIX4.0D and I added a Firewall hard disk
>

>Defering I/O (errno 5) for block(0x227b30, 0x227b30) on device 8,18438

This is probably due to scsi tag que depth.
The default in DU4 is 32 and for Fireball 8

add to /etc/ddr_dbase

SCSIDEVICE
    #
    # NOTE: Quantum fireball
    #
    Type = disk
    Name = "QUANTUM" "FIREBALL"
    #
    PARAMETERS:
        TagQueueDepth       = 8

and run ddr_config to update the kernel.


>
>The same message repeated itself with different blocks. The device
>8,18438 is the hard disk and this problem also happened about 3 months
>ago. The console could not detect the Hard Disk (addr B/2/0) but instead
>showed an unknown device from B/2/0 to B/2/7. Running fsck seemed to
>clear the problem but is there a way to scan the entire hard disk for
>bad sectors without removing any existing data?
Getting all units B/2/* usually indicates a bad SCSI chain 
(terminator, contacts...)

You can use the scu utility to scan the disk.
