The error can for example be seen when running the following example:

(*******************************)
program main(input,output);
var
 i:integer;

begin
 writeln('ln(2)=',ln(2):20:18);
 for i:=1 to 40 do
  writeln('i=',i:2,' 2^i=',exp(i*ln(2)):10:2);
end.
(******************************************)

It gives the output listed at the bottom of the message. Matlab and the
corresponding C-program on DEC gives  ln(2)=0.69314718055995 and so does the
IBM Pascal compiler on AIX. Also note that integer powers of 2 should be
integers (and even numbers).

I know that it is rare to depend on the accuracy above 8 significant
figures, but in my case I have a sum of a lot of positive and negative
numbers that almost cancel each other out. Each number is created by taking
the logarithm of some numbers, doing some operations on them and then using
exp() on the result. On our old IBM this program gave reliable results, but
when moved to DEC the results are meaningless.

I'm using OSF1 V4.0. I don't know what version of Pascal compiler it is, but
it has the same age as the OS.

Thank you

Morten

