I'm not the 'cc' driver expert, but I did ask around...

I think that you want to use .s files for Assembly source
code, not .S. The .s files are mentioned in the cc(1) man
page, but there's no mention of .S.

Here's what I found out from one of the experts:

 The .S suffix has a special meaning to the driver,
 which tells it to run umerge on the file first.
 Because of the -O option setting, it doesn't do the umerge, but the
 first thing it tries to do is a ugen, which fails.
 It considers the .S file an intermediate file which it can delete.

 So, the bottom line is, don't use the .S suffix.

I believe that even if the above did not happen, the .S suffix
will not be recognised as being Assembly source code. So, even
if the file is not deleted, I don't expect that it would give
you your expected results.

Let me know if you need more info.

-Jeff
