In article <36EF9B7D.5852128F@ev.co.yu>,
Nikola Milutinovic  <Nikola.Milutinovic@ev.co.yu> wrote:
>1. DNS3.ev.co.yu, the secondary DNS for all our zones, was upgraded to a new
>BIND version. And then it started reporting the following errors:
>
>named[334]: owner name "NW_Admin.ev.co.yu" IN (secondary) is invalid -
>proceeding anyway

Underscores are invalid in hostnames.  Recent versions of BIND reject them
by default on the primary server, and warn about them on secondary
servers.  The "check-names" option in the named configuration file can be
used to change this behavior.

>2. We have a news server, which allows all users from our domain to access news.
>In order to verify that the host is in *.ev.co.yu domain, news server maps IP
>into the name and checks. Now I have ran into the following error:
>
>nnrpd[24467]: ? cant gethostbyaddr 192.168.50.2 Not owner -- using IP address
>for access
>nnrpd[24467]: 192.168.50.2 connect
>nnrpd[24467]: 192.168.50.2 no_permission

Is this a Solaris system?  Have you put "dns" in the "hosts:" line of
/etc/nsswitch.conf?  If not, it won't use DNS for host name/address
resolution.

