Subject: Re: Setting up alternate boot disk?
Date: 1999/03/09
Message-ID: <01be6a18$fe7b5ed0$d53ec69b@leicester>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 452926124
References: <36E43AD6.8CA49B74@groupz.net>
Organization: Imperial College
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

It is just a matter of formatting and copying the original disk to the new.

Use "disklabel" to partition the new disk with, at least, a root and swap
partition - then use "newfs" to format them, apart from swap. You can, of
course, have partitions for /usr, /tmp, /var if you want to dispense with
the old versions too. A bootblock is the default for newfs.

Then you can mount each formatted partition in turn on to /mnt for copying.
Use the commands "dump" and "restore", or "vdump" and "vrestore", in a pipe
something like "dump 0f - / | ( cd /mnt; restore xf - )" for copying.

After copying the partitions mount the root partition again on /mnt. Edit
lines in /mnt/etc/fstab and /mnt/etc/rc.config pointing to the old disk -
usually just a matter of changing the SCSI ident.

Then boot from the new disk.

Good luck.
-- 

     Jeff Wilson
     London ~ UK


