So far, so good.

>         The root file system is then restored from tape to the partition.
> The system is then rebooted, but fails with the message stating "osf_boot is
> not found", or something to that effect.
> 
>         I must be missing something here. Is it because the root file system
> is being mounted as a ufs FS, and thus unable to locate osf_boot file?

Well, this should make no difference. BTW, how did you create a backup? With
"vdump", I hope.

> Also is "disklabel -rw" enough to make a disk bootable?
> 
>         I would be grateful for any pointers.

These are the steps, as described in System Administration, for converting UFS
to AdvFS. They assume two disks, but we could imagine a Digital UNIX
installation CD and a tape with "vdump 0 /" of the previous machine.

orig:/# vdump 0C /

copy:/# disklabel ... (tailor your partitions)
copy:/# mkfdmn -r -t rz26 /dev/rz0a root_domain
copy:/# mkfset root_domain root

copy:/# mount -t advfs root_domain#root /mnt
copy:/# cd /mnt
copy:/# vrestore -x

copy:/# disklabel -r /dev/rrz0a > /tmp/rz0.lbl
copy:/# disklabel -t advfs -r -R /dev/rrz2a /tmp/rz0.lbl

copy:/# (edit /mnt/etc/fstab - set to AdvFS)
copy:/# mkdir -p /mnt/etc/fdmns/root_domain
copy:/# ln -s /dev/rz0a /mnt/etc/fdmns/root_domain/rz0a

copy:/# umount /mnt
copy:/# halt

Try booting then.

Nix.



