In article <36DC3116.29F96411@msoe.edu>, ian reinhart geiser <geiseri@msoe.edu> writes:

>    How can one detect hardware on a server?

Lots of ways.  You can look at the boot messages and see
everything that the kernel found when it booted.  For
SCSI devices that might have show up later, you can use
scu(8) to scan the busses and see what is there:

# scu scan edt
# scu show edt

The drivers for most character special files will identify
the associated device when the right ioctl(2) is used.  The
file command will do this, so you can associate special
files to the device.  Some drivers in early V4 versions
didn't do this and the system would crash as a result.

dxsysinfo, will get some configuration information.

>    I am use to Linux where you have dmesg and
>    the proc file system.  Dose Digital Unix have
>    this?

If dmesg is what I remember, the contents of /var/adm/messages
contains a copy of the boot messages.  You can also get these
using DECevent and uerf(8).  In the case of uerf(8) you want
record type #300:

# uerf -R -r 300 | page

DECevent has a similar option.

The process file system is documented in the proc(4) manual
page.

>    -ian reinhart geiser
