Tcp/ip printing provides more flexibility by using print filters where
lpd remote printing does not.

Which print filter to use really depends on your specific printing needs.

A good starting point would be to use the ps_level1of filter.

-- generic tcp/ip setup instructions ---
These directions are for Compaq Tru64 (Digital) UNIX V4.0 and later.

        1) An IP name and address must be obtained for the printer. Once
           you have an IP address refer to the printer documentation to
           set it in the printer.

           Let say for this example the IP name is pr.local.com.

           If you can ping the printer continue on.

        2) Now you need to know the socket address for that printer.
Known
           socket address are:


                        Digital DEClaser 3500     10001
                                DEClaser 5100         10001
                                LN15                     3001
                                LN17                    2501
                                LN20                    35
                                LN40                    35
                        HP Printers                     9100
                        Lexmark Printers                9100
                        Xerox Printers                  2000, 2501

           As ROOT edit the /etc/services file and create a socket name
           for your printer. Example entry for socket 2501.

                port_2501       2501/tcp

           I chose the name port_2501 but any name can be used.

        3) Now that the services file has been updated, restart the inetd

           deamon by sending it a SIGHUP. This needs to be done to reload

           the updated services files. eg:

                # ps ax | grep inetd
                  446 ??       I        0:00.17 /usr/sbin/inetd
                  966 ttyp2    S  +     0:00.01 grep inetd
                # kill -HUP 446

          446 is the process ID number and will not be the same.

          ( You could also use the command "rcinet restart" )

        4) Now use lprsetup to create the print queue and make the
necessary
           printcap/printfilter changes. For this example the ln03r
printer
           was selected.

           Edit the printcap and change the lp value to:

                :lp=@pr.local.com/port_2501:\

           The ip name and port name were defined in the previous steps.

           Change the ct setting to `:ct=tcp:\'.

        5) If everything went well you should be able to print.

Example of a completed printcap entry:

pr|lp:\
        :ct=tcp:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/ln03rof:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lperr_pr:\
        :lp=@pr.local.com/port_2501:\
        :mx#0:\
        :of=/usr/lbin/ln03rof:\
        :pl#66:\
        :pw#80:\
        :rw:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/print/pr:\
        :xf=/usr/lbin/xf:

Joe wrote:

> I'm trying to add a laserjet III Si with jetdirect card to dunix 4.0d
> ok...
> Is the 3Si supposed to be in PCL or PS mode?
> What should I use for the if:? of:?
>
> hpnp under sunos 4.1.3 worked as a total no-brainer, one queue and the
> printer knew how to handle ps and ascii text.
