> Hi all.
> 
> Here is what I would like to do. I'm gonna create several account for in-bound
> FTP. User with USER/PASS for those account will be able to leave files on those
> account via FTP.
> 
> Then I'll create one "pick-up" account. User with that USER/PASS will pick up
> files. There will be a cron job, which will transfer files from "in-bound"
> accounts to the "pick-up" account.
> 
> Well, my problem is: how to detect that the file has been transferred?
> 
> The idea of testing a lock on the file, looked promissing, but I have to do it
> from a shell script (zsh). "cp" doesn't care if the file is under a
> lock/writing. I wouldn't want to implement any kind of "heuristic behavior" - IF
> filesize unchanged for past 15 min THEN copy.
> 
> Any ideas?

 A tool called lockfile comes with procmail. Maybe this is what you're
 looking for?

NAME
     lockfile - conditional semaphore-file creator

SYNOPSIS
     lockfile -sleeptime | -r retries |
          -l locktimeout | -s suspend | -! | -ml | -mu | filename
     ...

...
EXAMPLES
     Suppose you want to  make  sure  that  access  to  the  file
     "important"  is serialised, i.e. no more than one program or
     shell  script  should  be  allowed  to   access   it.    For
     simplicity's  sake, let's suppose that it is a shell script.
     In this case you could solve it like this:
          ...
          lockfile important.lock
          ...
          access_"important"_to_your_hearts_content
          ...
          rm -f important.lock
          ...
     Now if all the scripts that access "important"  follow  this
     guideline,  you will be assured that at most one script will
     be executing between the `lockfile' and the `rm' commands.
...



