ian reinhart geiser wrote in message <36DC3116.29F96411@msoe.edu>...
>    How can one detect hardware on a server?
>    I have an AlphaServer 2000 and am wondering
>    how to get some of the hardware stats.
>
>    I am use to Linux where you have dmesg and
>    the proc file system.  Dose Digital Unix have
>    this?
>
>    thanks
>
>    -ian reinhart geiser
>
Depends what you want to detect, most things get logged at boot time, so you
will see a message in the binary error-log entry corresponding to your last
boot (uerf will read this logfile)

If you want to detect network hardware, then on a newish Digital Unix (can't
say I noticed it much before 4.0b but maybe it was there) use ifconfig -a,
scsi stuff can be found with scu, and I suppose the generic answer is sizer,
which will tell you about processors, graphics cards and other things.

Usually with a 2000 its just easier to open the box.

Hope this helps,

Adam




